  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconFill">M0,0 M24,24 M11.2,3.6 L18.9,11.3 L12.4,17.8 C11,19.2 8.6,19.2 7.2,17.8 L5.4,16 C4,14.6 4,12.2 5.4,10.8 Z M4.5,11.7 H16.8 M20.6,14.8 C21.6,16.2 22.1,17.2 22.1,18.1 A1.9,1.9 0 0 1 18.3,18.1 C18.3,17.2 18.9,16.2 20.6,14.8 Z</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconPicker">M0,0 M24,24 M19.9,4.1 C21,5.2 21,6.9 19.9,8 L17.6,10.3 L13.7,6.4 L16,4.1 C17.1,3 18.8,3 19.9,4.1 Z M12.6,7.5 L16.5,11.4 L8.3,19.6 C7.5,20.4 6.3,20.8 5.2,20.6 L3.8,21.4 L2.6,20.2 L3.4,18.8 C3.2,17.7 3.6,16.5 4.4,15.7 Z</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconGradient">M0,0 M24,24 M4.5,5 H19.5 V19 H4.5 Z M19.5,5 L4.5,19</StreamGeometry>
  <!--
    The bone: the classic two-lobed shaft, on the diagonal the rest of the
    rail points along.

    It replaces IconMove, which this button wore as a placeholder through two
    releases — the rail is icon-only until it is dragged past 150px, so two
    identical four-way arrows sat side by side and the Bone tool was on
    screen and unfindable.

    The obvious replacement was the octahedral bone ArmatureOverlayPainter
    draws on the canvas, which has the virtue of matching the chrome. Drawn
    and looked at, it was a dart — near enough to IconPen's nib to reintroduce
    the same confusion one neighbour along. Recognisable beats consistent
    here: nothing else in the rail is a dumbbell, and that is the whole job of
    an icon.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconBone">M0,0 M24,24 M8.5,15.5 L15.5,8.5 M7.22,17.5 a2.1,2.1 0 1 0 4.2,0 a2.1,2.1 0 1 0 -4.2,0 M4.4,14.68 a2.1,2.1 0 1 0 4.2,0 a2.1,2.1 0 1 0 -4.2,0 M15.4,9.32 a2.1,2.1 0 1 0 4.2,0 a2.1,2.1 0 1 0 -4.2,0 M12.58,6.5 a2.1,2.1 0 1 0 4.2,0 a2.1,2.1 0 1 0 -4.2,0</StreamGeometry>
  <!-- Two offset L-brackets crossing, the way a crop mark is drawn on
       paper and the way every application that has this tool draws it. -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconCrop">M0,0 M24,24 M6.5,2.5 V17.5 H21.5 M2.5,6.5 H17.5 V21.5</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconMove">M0,0 M24,24 M12,2.8 V21.2 M2.8,12 H21.2 M12,2.8 L9.6,5.2 M12,2.8 L14.4,5.2 M12,21.2 L9.6,18.8 M12,21.2 L14.4,18.8 M2.8,12 L5.2,9.6 M2.8,12 L5.2,14.4 M21.2,12 L18.8,9.6 M21.2,12 L18.8,14.4</StreamGeometry>
  <!--
    The pen: a nib on the same diagonal the other tools point along, with the
    slit and the ink hole that make it read as a nib rather than as an arrow at
    16px. Drawn along an axis from the back edge to the tip so the two long
    sides converge symmetrically — a hand-placed quadrilateral looks bent at
    this size and there is no way to un-see it once noticed.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconPen">M0,0 M24,24 M17.8,2.8 L21.2,6.2 L11.4,14.8 L6.1,17.9 L9.3,12.6 Z M6.1,17.9 L10.3,13.7 M14.5,8.4 a1.1,1.1 0 1 0 0.01,0</StreamGeometry>
  <!--
    Width: a line that swells and thins along its length, with the two arrows
    that say which way a drag takes it. The lens shape is the whole idea in one
    glyph — a centreline with a width at every point, which is what a Lightbox
    stroke has always been.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconWidth">M0,0 M24,24 M3.5,12 C8,7 16,7 20.5,12 C16,17 8,17 3.5,12 Z M12,4.2 V6.6 M12,4.2 L10.4,5.8 M12,4.2 L13.6,5.8 M12,19.8 V17.4 M12,19.8 L10.4,18.2 M12,19.8 L13.6,18.2</StreamGeometry>

  <!--
    The two arrows. Both are the same pointer at the same angle, because they
    are the same gesture at two levels: the black arrow moves a whole line, the
    white one moves the points it is made of. Telling them apart is therefore
    the nodes and nothing else — a different silhouette would say they were
    unrelated tools, which is the opposite of true.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconArrow">M0,0 M24,24 M7,3.2 L7,17.8 L10.8,14.1 L13.2,19.9 L15.6,18.9 L13.3,13.3 L18.6,13.1 Z</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconPoints">M0,0 M24,24 M5.4,2.8 L5.4,14.6 L8.5,11.6 L10.5,16.3 L12.4,15.5 L10.4,10.9 L14.7,10.7 Z M15.6,14.6 H18.6 V17.6 H15.6 Z M9.4,18.6 H12.4 V21.6 H9.4 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!--
    Shape variants. Plain outlines: a shape tool draws exactly the outline it
    shows, so anything more decorative would be describing something the tool
    does not do.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconShapeLine">M0,0 M24,24 M4.6,19.4 L19.4,4.6</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconShapeRect">M0,0 M24,24 M4.5,6.5 H19.5 V17.5 H4.5 Z</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconShapeEllipse">M0,0 M24,24 M12,12 m-8,0 a8,6.2 0 1 0 16,0 a8,6.2 0 1 0 -16,0</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconShapePolygon">M0,0 M24,24 M12,4.2 L19.6,9.7 L16.7,18.6 H7.3 L4.4,9.7 Z</StreamGeometry>

  <!--
    Select variants, and the system is the point: the DASH means selection, the
    shape means which one. That is what lets a fifth variant be drawn later
    without inventing a new visual language for it — and it is why the box and
    the ellipse here are not simply IconShapeRect and IconShapeEllipse reused.
    A solid outline is a shape the tool will draw; a dashed one is a region it
    will select, and an artist should never have to work out which from the
    tooltip.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconSelectBox">M0,0 M24,24 M4.5,6.5 H10.5 M13.5,6.5 H19.5 M19.5,6.5 V10.5 M19.5,13.5 V17.5 M19.5,17.5 H13.5 M10.5,17.5 H4.5 M4.5,17.5 V13.5 M4.5,10.5 V6.5</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconSelectEllipse">M0,0 M24,24 M19.5,12 A7.5,6 0 0 1 13.9,17.8 M12,18 A7.5,6 0 0 1 4.8,13.6 M4.5,12 A7.5,6 0 0 1 10.1,6.2 M12,6 A7.5,6 0 0 1 19.2,10.5</StreamGeometry>
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconSelectPolygon">M0,0 M24,24 M13.4,5.5 L17.6,8.6 M18.5,11.3 L16.8,16.2 M14.6,17.9 H9.4 M7.2,16.2 L5.5,11.3 M6.4,8.6 L10.6,5.5</StreamGeometry>
  <!--
    The lasso is the one variant drawn unbroken, and deliberately: it is a loop
    an artist draws by hand, so a hand-drawn loop with the tail still hanging
    off it says freehand in a way a dashed outline cannot.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconSelectLasso">M0,0 M24,24 M9.6,17.5 C6.1,16.7 3.9,14.5 3.9,12 C3.9,8.4 7.5,5.5 12,5.5 C16.5,5.5 20.1,8.4 20.1,12 C20.1,15.6 16.5,18.5 12,18.5 M9.6,17.5 C8.7,18.9 8.3,20.3 8.7,21.3</StreamGeometry>
  <!--
    The wand replaces an emoji (U+1FA84), which rendered in full colour beside
    eleven monoline glyphs on the platforms that had it and as a blank box on
    the ones that did not.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconSelectWand">M0,0 M24,24 M3.6,20.4 L13.4,10.6 M17.5,3.4 V9.6 M14.4,6.5 H20.6 M20.5,11.4 V14.6 M18.9,13 H22.1</StreamGeometry>

  <!--
    Forbidden: the answer to "why is nothing happening". The circle and slash
    rather than an octagon, because this is worn by the POINTER — it has to read
    at 16px over arbitrary artwork, and it is the one shape every platform
    already uses for "not here". An octagon is a road sign; this is a refusal.
  -->
  <StreamGeometry x:Key="IconForbidden">M0,0 M24,24 M12,12 m-8.5,0 a8.5,8.5 0 1 0 17,0 a8.5,8.5 0 1 0 -17,0 M6,6 L18,18</StreamGeometry>

